# Chapter 4, fifth part: Global, Page and Global, Typeface.
# Part of the chapter source.

.h2 Page [format-page]
.ix Global Page
.ix Page layout
.ix Page layouts, the seven
.ix Global Page Layout
.ix Page layout settings
.ix Layout of the page
Global, Page sets the shape of the printed page: the paper, the four
margins, the page number tag and, for the title page, how far down the page
the text starts.  There are seven layouts, one panel each: the Global Page
Layout, which holds for every page, and six panels for special pages (the
title page, the table of contents, the first page, the even pages, the odd
pages and the index).  The Preview and the Printer use the paper, the margins
and the page number tag of the Global Page Layout and the Vertical Centering
of the Title Page Layout.
The Table of Contents Page Layout is kept with the document but the page of
contents that a print can make ({n:print-contents}) has the margins of the
other pages.

.h3 How to reach the command [format-page-reach]
.ix Page, reaching the command
.ix Page layout settings, hint line
Press {k:F12}, choose Global and then Page (the second entry; the hint line
reads "Page layout settings"), as described in {x:format-global-menu}.  The
first panel is the Global Page Layout.
.pic pictures/format-page-global.png 5 Global Page Layout.
The panels are a ring, like the fourteen of Global, Format: {k:PgUp} goes to
the next layout and {k:PgDn} to the previous one, and the ring wraps from
the last panel to the first.  {k:Ins} accepts all seven at once and
{k:Esc} none ({n:format-panel-keys}).
The panels follow one another from Global Page Layout to Index Page Layout.
Between them come the layouts of the title page, the table of contents, the
first page, the even pages and the odd pages.

.h3 The fields [format-page-fields]
.ix Left Margin
.ix Right Margin
.ix Top Margin
.ix Bottom Margin
.ix Page Number Tag
.ix Vertical Centering
.pic pictures/format-page-title.png 5 Title Page Layout.
Every field is a text field (a row of dots) unless the table says that it
is a choice.  Lengths are inches with up to three decimals, such as 1.250.
.table 21 12 11 13
Field | Values | Default | Panel
Left Margin | length | 1.000 | all seven
Right Margin | length | 1.000 | all seven
Top Margin | length | 1.000 | all seven
Bottom Margin | length | 1.000 | all seven
Header | text | empty | all seven
Footer | text | empty | all seven
Header Indent | length | 0.000 | all seven
Footer Indent | length | 0.000 | all seven
Header Whitespace | length | 0.500 | all seven
Footer Whitespace | length | 0.500 | all seven
Page Number Tag | text | \1\ | all seven
Paper Size | a list | Letter | Global
Paper Bin | information | 1 | all seven
Vertical Centering (%) | 0 to 100 | 33 | Title
.end
A panel of a special page shows, in square brackets, the value that it takes
from the Global Page Layout for every field except the Header and the Footer,
whose empty field means no header or footer on that page.  Put a value in
the field to give the page a value of its own.  Paper Bin is an information
field: the cursor skips it and it cannot be changed.
{b:Margins.}  Left, Right, Top and Bottom Margin are the distance from
the edge of the paper to the text.  A value is a number with an optional
decimal point and at most three decimals; a letter, a sign or a fourth
decimal is refused ({n:format-messages}).  There is no upper limit on the
panel, but when the margins leave less than 2 inches of width or of height
for the text, the Printer uses 1.000 inch for the two margins of that
direction instead.

{b:Page Number Tag.}  The first character after the first backslash says
how the page numbers are written; a tag without a backslash, or one whose
first letter is not one of those below, makes Arabic numbers.
.table 14 40
Tag | Page numbers
\1\ | Arabic: 1, 2, 3 (the default).
\i\ | Lower case roman: i, ii, iii.
\I\ | Upper case roman: I, II, III.
\a\ | Lower case letters: a, b, c.
\A\ | Upper case letters: A, B, C.
.end
A roman numeral shows the numbers 1 to 3999; the page number 4000 is
written 4000, whatever the tag.
The number is printed alone, centered at the foot of every page.  The title
page has no page number, and the numbering starts at 1 on the page after it
({n:format-set-pagebreaks}).  A Page Number marker in the text prints the
number of the page it is on, at that place.

{b:Vertical Centering.}  On the Title Page Layout, a whole number from 0 to
100 (anything else is rejected): the percent of the way down the page
at which the blocks of the title page start.

.h3 Page breaks and the title page [format-set-pagebreaks]
.ix Page breaks
.ix Keep Block on One Page
.ix Title page
.ix Pagination
A page break happens in print when a Page Break marker says so (a page, an odd
page or an even page: {n:markers}), when an Orientation marker changes the
page, at each headline of the Page Break Level of the Global Format settings
or of a higher level ({n:format-fields-spacing}), at the end of the title
page, before the page of endnotes, and where the text fills a page.  A
headline is kept with the lines that follow it (at
least four lines of space are needed at the foot of a page, or the headline
goes to the next page).

{b:The title page.}  In a structured document the blocks that come before the
first section (the text that Edit, Title edits: {n:edit}) are the title page.
It is printed on a page of its own with no page number, its blocks are placed
a given percent of the way down (the Vertical Centering of the Title Page
Layout, 33 % by default), and the page numbers start at 1 on the next page.

.h3 Paper size [format-page-paper]
.ix Paper Size
.ix Paper list
.ix Paper, width and height
.ix Letter
.ix Legal
.ix A4
Paper Size on the Global Page Layout is a choice, with the list below.
Choose with {k:Left}, {k:Right} or {k:Space}, or open the list with {k:F2}
or {k:Alt-+} ({n:format-panel-keys}).  The Printer makes a page of the width
and the height of the paper chosen; a document with no choice has Letter.
.table 32 12 13
Paper | Width in | Height in
Letter (8 1/2 x 11 in) | 8.500 | 11.000
Legal (8 1/2 x 14 in) | 8.500 | 14.000
Fanfold A4 (8 1/2 x 12 in) | 8.500 | 12.000
Half Letter (5 1/2 x 8 1/2 in) | 5.500 | 8.500
Computer Paper (14 x 11 in) | 14.000 | 11.000
Tabloid (11 x 17 in) | 11.000 | 17.000
Newspaper (17 x 22 in) | 17.000 | 22.000
A3 (297 x 420 mm) | 11.693 | 16.535
A4 (210 x 297 mm) | 8.268 | 11.693
A5 (148 x 210 mm) | 5.827 | 8.268
B4 (250 x 352 mm) | 9.843 | 13.858
B5 (176 x 250 mm) | 6.929 | 9.843
Monarch Envelope (3 7/8 x 7 1/2 in) | 3.875 | 7.500
Business Envelope (4 1/8 x 9 1/2 in) | 4.125 | 9.500
DL Envelope (110 x 220 mm) | 4.331 | 8.661
C5 Envelope (162 x 229 mm) | 6.378 | 9.016
Number 9 Envelope (3 7/8 x 8 7/8 in) | 3.875 | 8.875
C6/5 Envelope (114 x 229 mm) | 4.488 | 9.016
C6, Yougata 2 Envelope (114 x 162 mm) | 4.488 | 6.378
Yougata 4 Envelope (105 x 235 mm) | 4.134 | 9.252
Custom (the size of the document) | its width | its length
.end
A document whose paper is none of these shows Custom, and the entry then
reads the real size of the document, for example Custom (5 1/8 x 5 1/8 in) for
a square card, or Custom (100 x 150 mm) for a postcard (the shorter side
first, in millimetres when both sides are whole millimetres and
in inches otherwise).  A size is matched by its numbers either way round, so a
paper stored with its sides turned is the same paper, and a stored size that
belongs to no named paper is never shown with the name of another one.  An
envelope that is chosen in the list is stored with its long side as the width,
because it lies in the printer that way: the Preview and the print show it
with the long side across.  Choosing Custom keeps the size that the document
has.  Leaving the panel with Ins when nothing was changed leaves the size as
it was.

{b:An envelope needs more than its size.}  When you choose an envelope the
Editor also sets what an envelope needs, once, at the choice: the Left Margin
becomes 0.276 inch (7 mm), the Top Margin the value that puts the first line
7 mm from the top edge (0.219 inch for the type of 14 points), and the Right
and Bottom Margin 0.500, so that the first glyph box of the first line stands
7 mm from the left and the top edge of the envelope; the type becomes 14
points when the document names no size of its own.  A margin that
you changed in the same visit of the panel keeps the value that you typed.
Choosing another paper later leaves the margins and the type as they are, and
{k:Ctrl-Z} takes the whole choice back in one step.  The Printer sets in 14
points any page that is an envelope: a page whose sides are each within 0.3
inch of an envelope of the list, or whose long side is at least twice its
short side (the short side 2.5 to 5 inches, the long side at most 11.5
inches), so that an envelope that measures a quarter inch more than the list
is also set in 14 points.  The print system is asked not to scale such a page
(the option {c:print-scaling=none} of
{c:lp}), because a scaled page has smaller type and a corner that has moved.
The PDF and the arguments of {c:lp} are checked by tests; the result on
paper is not.  If an envelope still prints wrongly, open the PDF that the Printer
made (the Output File of the Print panel): when the PDF is right, with a page
9 inches across, type of 14 points and the sender near the corner, the
difference is in the print system, and the media of the printer for
envelopes (the envelope size, with the manual feed as the paper source) is
the first setting to look at.

.h3 Header and Footer [format-page-headers]
.ix Header
.ix Footer
.ix Running head
.ix Page layout, header and footer
The Header and the Footer are one line of text with up to three parts, the
left, the center and the right part, separated by a bar:
.code
Chapter | \page\ | \section\
.end
Without a bar the line is the center part; with one bar it is the left part and
the right part.  {c:\page\} is the Page Number marker and {c:\section\} the
Section marker: the number of the page, in the style of the Page Number Tag,
and the number and the headline of the section ({n:markers}).  The text is plain
letters, digits and signs; at most three parts are allowed, and a bar cannot be
part of the text.

The Section marker has three forms, so that a header can show the section in
the way you want:
.table 18 12 27
Form | Prints | Example on a page in section 3.2
{c:\section\} | The number and the title (the default) | 3.2 Search
{c:\sectionnumber\} | The number only | 3.2
{c:\sectiontitle\} | The title only | Search
.end
The number is the tag of the headline as it is printed at the headline, with
its punctuation, so a headline that is printed as {c:Part III.} gives
{c:Part III.}.  The page shows the section whose headline is the first one on
the page, or, when the page has no headline, the section of the page before.  A
headline that has no tag (the numbering is off for its level) gives its title
for all three forms, and the pages before the first headline show nothing.  A
document that was made with a Section marker that has no choice prints the
number and the title.

A Section marker that stands in the text of a document, and not in a header or
a footer, is changed like any marker: put the cursor on it and press
{k:Ctrl-F12}.  Its panel has one field, {b:Show}, with the same three choices:
Number and Title, Number Only and Title Only ({fig:format-section-marker}).
.pic pictures/format-section-marker.png 5 The panel of a Section marker, with the choice Title Only. [format-section-marker]

Every one of the seven panels has the two fields.  A page has the header and
the footer of the layout that suits it: the Title Page Layout for the title
page, the Table of Contents Page Layout for the pages of the contents, the First
Page Layout for the first page of the text, the Even Page Layout for the even
pages and the Odd Page Layout for the others.  A layout that has no header of
its own takes the one of the Global Page Layout, and the panel shows that one
in square brackets; leave the brackets as they are to keep it so.  An empty
field on a layout means no header there, which is how a title page has none.
Header Whitespace is the distance between the header and the text, and Footer
Whitespace the distance between the text and the footer.  The Printer uses the
values of the Global Page Layout and keeps both distances inside the margins.
Header Indent and Footer Indent move the header and the footer in from the left
margin of the text: the left part starts that far from the margin, the middle
part is centered in what is left, and the right part stays at the right margin.
A layout that has no indent of its own takes the one of the Global Page Layout.

A header or a footer that holds more than this, such as bold letters or another
marker, is shown on the panel and cannot be changed there.  A document in which
no layout has any text prints as before: the page number is at the foot of every
page but the title page ({n:print-elements}).
.pic pictures/format-page-headfoot.png 5 The Global Page Layout of a document that has a header and a footer.
.pic pictures/print-headfoot-page.png 5 A page printed with that header and footer.

.h3 What the panel checks [format-page-checks]
.ix Page layout, checks
.ix Page Layout, message
All seven panels are read when {k:Ins} is pressed.  A length must be a number
such as 1.000 or 1.5 (digits, an optional point, at most three decimals), and
Vertical Centering a whole number from 0 to 100.  The empty field is always
good.  When a value is wrong, a box titled Page Layout shows which field and
why (the messages are in {n:format-messages}), and the panel that holds the
field is shown again; nothing is applied until every panel is right.  The
Page Number Tag is not checked.  A Header or a Footer with more than three parts
or with a letter that is not plain is refused too.  After {k:Esc} nothing is
changed.

.h2 Typeface [format-typeface]
.ix Global Typeface
.ix Typeface List
.ix Font list
.ix Font names
.ix Font numbers
Global, Typeface gives names to the twenty fonts of a document.  The
formats do not choose a font by name but by number, 1 to 20 (Headline Font
and Text Font on the Format panels, the Font of a named format and a Font
marker), and this panel gives the name of each number.  The name is also
what determines the face that the Printer uses ({n:format-set-typefaces}).

.h3 How to reach the command [format-typeface-reach]
Press {k:F12}, choose Global and then Typeface (the third entry; the hint
line reads "Font assignments"), as described in {x:format-global-menu}.  The
panel is the Typeface List.  It has no neighbors: {k:Ins} accepts and
{k:Esc} cancels.
.pic pictures/format-typeface.png 5 The Typeface List.

.h3 The Typeface List [format-typeface-list]
.ix Font 1 to Font 20
.ix Intro head
.ix Header/Footer, font name
.ix Level 1 head
.ix Level 2 head
The panel has twenty text fields, Font 1 to Font 20.  A field holds the name
of the font.  A name may be any text; the field scrolls when the name is
longer than the field.  The last row, Printer, is an information field: it
shows the printer name of the document and "Generic 10.0 pitch", and the
cursor skips it.  The names that a font has when nothing was changed are
these.
.table 10 20 27
Font | Default name | Used for
1 | Intro head | A name for the font of an introduction.
2 | Text | The default font of the text of every section.
3 | Header/Footer | A name for the font of headers and footers.
4 | Level 1 head | The default font of the headline of every section.
5 | Level 2 head | A name for the font of second level headlines.
6 to 20 | Font 6 to Font 20 | Free for your own use.
.end
The default headline font of the Global Format panel is font 4 and the
default text font is font 2.  The panels of Global, Format, Level and Named
show the fonts by their names, so a name changed here appears there at once;
the choice list of a font field is the twenty names.  The File menu's
Retrieve command can fetch the Typeface settings of another document, or of
a template, without its text ({n:file}).

The Document Manager has a list of its own, TeX Font Association ({n:str}),
that names the LaTeX package for each font number, for the user and not for
the document.  The Font field of the Keycap Format panel
({n:format-marker}) shows the names of that list.

.h3 Typefaces and the printed page [format-set-typefaces]
.ix Typefaces
.ix Fonts
.ix Font family
.ix Headline size
.ix Font marker
The formats pick a font by number: the Headline Font and Text Font fields of
the Global Format and Level panels, the Font field of the Named panel and the
Font marker inside a text ({n:markers}).  The panels show the font by its name,
and a level shows [name] for the font it takes from the global setting.

The screen does not use fonts.  The printed page has three kinds of face:
roman, sans serif and typewriter, at 11 points (14 points when the paper is an
envelope of the paper list, because an envelope is read from further away).  The
kind is chosen by the
{i:name} of the font, not by its number:
.table 12 48
Family | The name contains (any case)
Typewriter | program, code, courier, mono, typewriter, fixed, listing, source
Sans serif | head, title, sans, helv, arial, gothic (but not "Header")
Roman | anything else
.end
The words are looked for in this order, the typewriter ones first.  By the
default names Fonts 4 and 5 (Level 1 head, Level 2 head) and Font 1 (Intro
head) are sans serif, Font 2 Text and Font 3 Header/Footer are roman, and so
are the other fonts, and a font that you name Program is typewriter.  A name
that is changed changes the family.  A headline is set in a larger size by its
level: level 1 large, level 2 a little smaller, level 3 and deeper in the
size of the text.

.behavior A headline that takes the default font prints in the roman face,
  even though the panel shows Font 4, Level 1 head.  Choosing the default
  (Font 4) on the panel gives the default again.  Choose another font, or
  rename a font, to get a different family.

.caution A font name that contains one of the words that the Printer reads
  (program, code, courier, mono, typewriter, fixed, listing, source for
  the typewriter face; head, title, sans, helv, arial, gothic for the sans
  serif face) changes how the text looks in print.  Naming font 2 "Text
  (code)" would make all the text of the document typewriter text.
